.Dd $Mdocdate: November 24 2026 $
.Dt IMAPIDLE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm imapidle
.Nd watch IMAP mailboxes and run mbsync
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm imapidle
.Op Fl dvn
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Nm imapidle
.Fl -tunnel Ar section
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
logs into one or more IMAP accounts, waits for mailbox changes, and runs
.Sy mbsync
for the matching channel.
.Sy mbsync
itself runs once and exits.
.Nm
is the long-running side of that pair.
Without
.Fl d
the starting process logs
.Ql -> pid
and exits.
The pid in that line is the process that keeps running.
A failed fork is one error line from the starting process.
.Pp
The running daemon is a supervisor and a hub.
The hub holds the configuration, the state, and the schedule.
It runs
.Sy mbsync
and does not open an IMAP socket.
Each watched folder is a process that logs in and stays in
.Cm IDLE .
Each pulse account is a process that logs in and runs
.Cm STATUS
when the schedule asks.
A tracked mailbox on an account that has one of those processes
is checked with
.Cm STATUS
before
.Sy mbsync .
An account with no such process is synced on its timer.
.Fl n
is the same split, but it only logs the sync it would run.
.Nm ps
shows the supervisor as
.Ql imapidle: parent
and the hub as
.Ql imapidle: hub .
An idle process is
.Ql imapidle: idle
followed by its account and folder.
A pulse process is
.Ql imapidle: account
followed by its account.
.Pp
Each account is independent.
A bad password, a refused certificate, a dead server, or a broken
mailbox connection is logged and retried on that account.
A failed
.Sy mbsync
is logged and does not disable the account.
Five consecutive failures before the account has monitored a mailbox
pause it.
The first pause is 1800 seconds.
The failure count and the connect backoff are reset, and the account
is tried again when that pause ends.
Another five failures pause it for one and a half times as long,
rounded.
A step that would pass 12 hours is not taken, so the pause stays
on the last step before that.
.Dv SIGHUP
enables it on the next wake and the pause starts over at 1800 seconds.
A section that sets
.Va disabled
to
.Cm yes
is not connected.
It stays disabled until that value is removed and the file is
reloaded.
An account that has monitored a mailbox keeps retrying after a later
failure, with the wait growing by half each time up to 900 seconds.
Three authentication failures in a row wait one week instead.
.Sy imapidlectl(1)
.Ar account.name.next_try
schedules that login again.
Zero tries on the next loop.
A failing
.Va pass_cmd
uses its own wait.
The first retry is 60 seconds, and each later failure doubles that
wait up to 21600 seconds.
The error is logged on the first failure and again only after
86400 seconds.
The retries in between are quiet.
A later success logs that the command recovered, and the account
is not disabled.
An error line from that command, including an authorisation URL,
is the same failure.
The account is not connected and
.Sy mbsync
is not started.
It does not stop the other accounts, and it does not exit the process.
The process leaves the main loop only on
.Dv SIGTERM
or
.Dv SIGINT ,
or when a shutdown file is present.
Each open IDLE session is ended with DONE and then LOGOUT.
Each NOTIFY session is ended with LOGOUT.
A session that is already dead is closed without those commands.
In-flight
.Sy mbsync
processes finish, the state files are written, and the process
exits 0.
A child this process cannot reap is logged
.Ql gone
and does not hold that exit.
That wait is at least one second.
A shutdown file present at startup stops the process
before it opens a mailbox and before it daemonizes.
The same check runs again before each account and
before each further mailbox.
An IMAP wait ends when the file is present, so a login
already started does not run on into
.Sy IDLE .
An
.Sy mbsync
this process already started still finishes.
.Dv SIGHUP
re-reads the configuration file on the next wake of the loop,
within about 30 seconds, and does not exit.
A file that would be rejected at startup is logged and the
running accounts and pulse channels stay as they are.
An account whose server, port, user, secret, or channel changed
is disconnected and connected again.
Folder names, priorities, and resync times apply to the connection already open.
A folder that is new, and not already covered by
.Cm NOTIFY ,
is connected on that wake.
.Dv SIGINFO
prints the running state and does not exit.
With
.Fl d
each line goes to standard error.
A daemonized process writes the same lines to
.Xr syslog 3
instead.
The lines name each account and connection, each folder's priority
and resync time, when that folder was last synced, tracked
mailboxes, any
.Sy mbsync
child, and each pulse channel.
With
.Fl v
each tracked mailbox is listed on its own line.
A paused account is named disabled and shows how long it has left.
On a terminal, Ctrl-T sends this signal.
.Pp
A
.Ux
socket at
.Pa ~/var/run/imapidle.sock
answers
.Sy imapidlectl(1) .
The socket mode is 0600.
The directory is created mode 0700 when it is missing.
If the directory cannot be created, or the socket cannot
be opened, the process keeps running and answers no
control client.
One line in, then
.Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
lines, then the connection closes.
A name lists that node and every node under it.
A dot inside a name is written as two dots.
.Ar all
lists every node.
.Ar shutdown Ns = Ns 1
requests the same exit as
.Dv SIGTERM
and does not create a shutdown file.
.Ar pause Ns = Ns 1
pauses the way a pause file does.
.Ar pause Ns = Ns 0
clears that request and leaves a pause file in place.
Passwords, password commands, hook commands, and learned
hashes are not listed.
The shutdown file and the pause file still work.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c Ar file
Read
.Ar file
instead of the default configuration.
State files, including the certificate store, live in
.Pa ~/.local/imapidle/ .
A missing
.Pa known_hosts
or
.Pa learned
there is copied once from the same directory as
.Ar file .
.It Fl d
Stay in the foreground.
Do not double-fork.
Copy each log line to standard error as well as
.Xr syslog 3 .
Use this to watch a run.
.It Fl v
Log the IMAP keepalive lines.
Those are
.Ql * OK Still here
and the notice that
.Cm IDLE
was armed again.
They are omitted by default.
The first look at a host also logs the authentication
mechanisms that server offers, once.
It also logs the
.Va state_keep
pattern and, for each configured mailbox, whether a
sync-state failure would keep or remove it.
An
.Sy mbsync
error that is not a sync-state failure logs
.Ql not a sync-state error .
The flag applies when the process is a daemon and with
.Fl d .
.It Fl n
Watch mailboxes without writing state and without running
.Sy mbsync .
The configuration and
.Pa ~/.local/imapidle
are loaded into memory.
A sync that would have started is logged, then this process
waits the predicted number of seconds.
The wait is that average, or five seconds when no average
is stored, plus or minus about a quarter of the average.
The state directory is left untouched.
A pulse account is its own process.
That process logs in and runs
.Cm STATUS .
The process that supervises it does not open an IMAP socket.
Certificates are not checked or stored.
The control socket is not opened, so this process can run
beside another
.Nm .
.Fl -tunnel
cannot be combined with this flag.
.Dv SIGHUP
is ignored.
.Dv SIGTERM
or
.Dv SIGINT
stops the process.
.El
.Pp
An unknown certificate is stored in
.Pa known_hosts
and the connection continues.
The first look at a host logs one line named
.Ql [tls] .
The name is padded so its colon lines up with other messages.
The text gives the host number, as
.Ql 01.cert ,
then the host and port, the TLS version, the cipher, the cipher
length in bits
.Pq ci ,
the leaf public-key length in bits
.Pq skey ,
.Cm comp
and 0 or 1, then
.Cm fp
and
.Cm match ,
.Cm new ,
or
.Cm change .
.Cm comp
is 1 when that TLS session is using compression.
The record is one log line.
Broken to fit this page, it looks like:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[tls]   : 01.cert mail.example:993 TLSv1.2
    ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384 ci 256b skey 4096b
    comp 0 fp match
.Ed
.Pp
.Fl v
adds one companion line the first time a host is seen.
It lists the authentication mechanisms that server offered,
numbered like the certificate line
.Pq 01.auth ,
and later connections do not repeat it.
.Pp
The spaces before the colon are that padding.
With no accounts loaded the name column is eight characters, so
.Ql [tls]
has three spaces before the colon.
A longer account or channel name widens the column.
.Cm new
adds
.Ql [tls]: NN.cert +
and the fingerprint.
.Cm change
adds that line and one beginning with
.Ql -
and the previous fingerprint.
A later connection to the same host does not repeat them.
A replaced fingerprint stays in the file with the GMT time it was
retired.
One fingerprint per host has no retired time, and that one is current.
.Pp
The chain stored under
.Pa certs/
is checked on every handshake.
The connection continues either way.
Each of these is its own warning:
the certificate is expired or not yet valid, the server name does
not match, there is no
.Cm subjectAltName ,
the certificate is not X.509 v3, the public key is shorter than
2048 bits
.Pq 224 for an elliptic curve
or an unsuitable kind, or the signature uses MD5 or SHA-1.
An issuer that is not the next certificate, a signature that does
not verify, and a chain that
.Pa /etc/ssl/cert.pem
does not trust are warned about too.
Every problem found is logged.
The same
.Pa certs/
file stays quiet for 7 days after that report.
The times are kept in
.Pa cert-warn .
.Pp
Operational messages use
.Xr syslog 3
with ident
.Cm imapidle ,
options
.Cm cons,pid ,
and facility
.Cm mail .
A line about an account or an mbsync channel starts with that name
and a colon.
.Pp
.Sy mbsync
is executed as
.Pa /usr/local/bin/mbsync
followed by one
.Ar channel:box
argument per folder in the batch.
Folders of the same channel that become ready together are one process.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mbsync channel:INBOX channel:Archive
.Ed
.Pp
A configured
.Va hook
runs after that process exits 0.
One exit runs the command once, not once per folder.
The same command already running runs once more when it exits,
not as a second copy at the same time.
A hook is not an
.Sy mbsync
slot, and shutdown does not wait for it.
A non-zero exit does not run the hook.
A pause file does not cancel a hook whose
.Sy mbsync
already exited.
The command text is not logged.
.Pp
The process is started only after
.Nm
is the process that stays.
A child of the starter would be adopted by init.
.Pp
.Va tunnel
in the global section is a second way to run that command.
The default is
.Cm no ,
and
.Sy mbsync
is executed as above.
.Cm yes
writes a mode 0600 copy of the
.Sy mbsync
configuration under
.Pa ~/.local/imapidle/tunnel/
and runs
.Sy mbsync Fl c
on that copy.
The copy is removed when that process exits.
For each account this program can log into, the copy drops the host,
port, user, password, and TLS settings, and adds a
.Cm Tunnel
command,
.Cm TLSType None ,
and a
.Cm Timeout
of
.Va tunnel_timeout
seconds.
The default timeout is 3600.
.Sy mbsync
runs the command through
.Pa /bin/sh Fl c .
The command is this program,
.Fl -tunnel Ar section ,
and the same
.Fl c
file.
That process opens its own TLS session, logs in, writes
.Ql * PREAUTH ,
and copies bytes until one side closes.
Nothing else is written to that stream.
A channel with no matching section keeps its original account,
including its password.
If the copy cannot be built, the log says so and
.Sy mbsync
runs on its own configuration.
Turning
.Va tunnel
off, or back on, takes effect on the next
.Dv SIGHUP .
.Pp
.Fl -tunnel
reads one
.Cm idle ,
.Cm account ,
or
.Cm pulse
section, logs in, and exits.
It does not watch other accounts and it does not become a daemon.
Standard output is the IMAP stream.
.Pp
A ready event starts a 5 second wait on that channel.
Other ready events for the same channel during those 5 seconds join the
same invocation.
The 5 seconds are not restarted by a later event.
The child then waits for the largest
.Va wait
of the folders in the batch, then replaces itself with
.Sy mbsync .
When the process is forked, the line names the channel, the slot,
.Cm start ,
a
.Cm sha1 -b
token of the argument list, the pid in five columns, and the
mailboxes.
When that command has finished before, the line also gives
.Cm guestimate Ns ending at YYYYMMDD HH:MM:SS .
N is the average real seconds of the command, rounded.
The clock is local and is N seconds after the fork.
The slot counts from 0 in base 36, digits 0-9 and a-z.
When that process exits, a
.Cm stop
line repeats the slot, the token, the pid, and the mailboxes, then
.Cm ret
and the status, or
.Cm sig
and the signal number.
A slot at or past
.Va connections
means more
.Sy mbsync
processes were started than that cap allows.
The same line gives how many times that command has finished, this
run against the average real seconds, and this run against the
average resident set, in kilobytes.
With
.Fl v
it also shows user and system seconds, page reclaims, page faults,
block input, block output, and both context-switch counts, each as
this run against its average.
Every one of those figures is totaled either way.
A zero status is informational.
Any other exit is a warning.
A sync-state failure is handled separately.
That is an unreadable or incomplete
.Pa .mbsyncstate ,
or a UIDVALIDITY change
.Sy mbsync
will not recover.
The local mailbox is removed and that one box is synced again,
so the server copy is downloaded.
.Va state_keep
in
.Pa [track]
is a case-insensitive regular expression of mailbox names
that are left in place.
The default is
.Ql (?:^|/)(?:draft|sent)[^/]*$
which matches a final component that starts with
.Cm draft
or
.Cm sent .
That keeps
.Cm Drafts ,
.Cm Sent ,
.Cm Sent Items ,
.Cm Sent Messages ,
and
.Cm Sent Mail ,
including
.Ql [Gmail]/Sent Mail .
.Cm Trash ,
.Cm Calendar ,
and
.Cm INBOX
do not match, so those mailboxes are removed.
Any other
.Sy mbsync
error is only the warning, and the mailbox is not removed.
The IMAP loop does not wait for
.Sy mbsync
to finish.
.Pp
.Va connections
in
.Pa [track]
limits how many of those processes run at once.
The default is 2.
When the limit is above 1 and a pulse mailbox is due to sync,
one slot stays with the named folders and pulse uses the others.
A due
.Cm STATUS
does not take a slot.
One long named sync does not hold every pulse mailbox.
With one slot, a pulse sync waits until that named sync is done.
When no pulse sync is due, hot folders may use every slot.
Tracked mailboxes and pulse mailboxes leave one slot free when
the limit is above 1 and no named sync is using it.
A pause file stops new
.Sy mbsync
processes until it is removed.
IMAP keeps running.
The loop sleeps a second while that file is present,
so a due mailbox does not busy-poll.
A shutdown file uses the same finish path as
.Dv SIGTERM .
.Pp
An IMAP server can refuse a login when this user and address
already hold every connection it allows.
.Nm
remembers how many of its own sessions were open for that
server, port, and user, and does not open another until a week
has passed.
It then tries one extra session.
A refusal keeps the remembered count and waits another week.
A success raises that count by one.
The number in the server message is stored and is not the cap,
because other programs share the server's limit.
When that number is above 1, the remembered count is kept at
most one below it, so
.Sy mbsync
can log in as the same user.
An idle folder past that count is closed, lowest priority
first, and waits for the same week.
A sync refused for this reason is tried again in about ten
seconds.
The refusal is not a connect failure and does not shorten the
loop sleep.
The counts are kept in
.Pa ~/.local/imapidle/conn-limit .
This is separate from
.Va connections ,
which limits
.Sy mbsync
processes.
.Sy SIGINFO
shows the count, when the next try is due, and the server's
number when one was given.
.Sy imapidlectl(1)
shows those values and can set the count and the time of the
next try.
See
.Sy imapidle.conf(5) .
.Pp
One or more
.Cm pulse
sections each name accounts and a
.Va sweep .
Each of those accounts opens one IMAP connection for
.Cm LIST
and
.Cm STATUS
and does not enter
.Cm IDLE .
Every mailbox that list returns is synced as its own
.Pa mbsync channel:box .
An empty mailbox uses the tracked empty interval and the weekly
confirmation, not
.Va sweep .
A mailbox that has mail waits
.Va sweep
seconds after its own
.Sy mbsync
exits.
The first time that mailbox is seen with mail, the wait adds the
same quarter spread used by backoff.
Each mailbox draws its own spread, so a herd of new mailboxes
does not sync in the same second.
A time saved in
.Pa pulse-last
is the start of that first wait when the mailbox is not already
in the track file.
If that time plus the spread wait is already past, the mailbox
syncs now.
One mailbox of a channel runs at a time.
A bare channel and a
.Ar channel:box
for that same channel do not run together.
See
.Sy imapidle.conf(5) .
.Pp
Mailboxes on an account channel that are not in that account's
.Va folderN
list are tracked.
Each one runs as its own
.Pa mbsync channel:box
so a long folder does not block a hot folder on that channel.
The first wait is 30 minutes, measured after
.Sy mbsync
exits.
Every three samples replace the wait.
No new mail doubles it.
When the new wait is longer, one quarter of the added seconds
is X.
A random value modulo X is added so those mailboxes do not wake
together.
A mailbox that is due does not busy-wait while its channel
is already syncing, or while every slot is in use.
A
.Cm STATUS
already waiting on the server is left to finish.
The loop sleeps, and a child exit wakes it.
The wait stays between 10 minutes and 6 hours.
With new mail the wait aims for about 5 messages and is at least
three times the average sync of that mailbox.
Before that run, the primary connection sends
.Cm STATUS
for
.Cm MESSAGES
and
.Cm UIDNEXT .
No messages, including the first answer, waits a day
and does not sync.
The first time that mailbox is empty, a confirmation is
set seven days out.
One quarter of those seven days is X, and a random value
modulo X is added, the same spread a longer tracked wait
uses.
A mailbox that is still empty when that time arrives runs
.Sy mbsync
once, in case mail was added on this machine.
After a quiet confirmation the mailbox waits a day to be
asked again, and another seven days plus that spread
before the next confirmation.
Mail in the
.Sy mbsync
summary leaves the empty class and follows the interval
above.
A count above zero, or a changed
.Cm UIDNEXT ,
runs
.Sy mbsync
and then follows the interval above.
A failed
.Cm STATUS
closes that connection and syncs the mailbox
instead of asking again immediately.
On shutdown the next wake, the interval,
.Cm UIDNEXT ,
and the empty confirmation time
are written with the time the next folder list is due.
A later start uses those clocks.
It still logs in, idles each configured folder, and syncs
those folders once.
The folder list and a tracked
.Cm STATUS
wait until the saved times.
A name containing a colon is remembered and not synced: the command
splits on the first colon.
A folder the server no longer lists is not tried again until a later
list contains it.
.Sh IDLE AND NOTIFY
If the server advertises
.Cm NOTIFY ,
.Nm
sends
.Dl NOTIFY SET (personal (MessageNew MessageExpunge MessageFlags))
on the connection it keeps.
Each configured folder keeps its own minimum time.
An event that names a mailbox schedules that folder.
An event that names none schedules every folder configured for the account.
.Pp
A capability list with neither
.Cm NOTIFY
nor
.Cm IDLE
keeps that one connection and does not select a folder.
Every
.Va poll
seconds, 60 when the account omits the key, it sends
.Cm STATUS
.Pq MESSAGES UIDNEXT
for each configured folder.
The first look records those counts.
A later change in the message count or
.Cm UIDNEXT
schedules
.Sy mbsync
the same way an
.Cm IDLE
event does.
The connection is not an
.Cm IDLE
session.
Shutdown logs it out and does not send
.Cm DONE .
.Pp
Otherwise
.Nm
opens one
.Cm IDLE
connection per folder
.Pq RFC 2177 .
While a connection is in
.Cm IDLE ,
.Nm
sends no other commands on it.
About every 30 seconds it sends
.Cm DONE
and issues
.Cm IDLE
again, which also discovers a dead server.
On
.Cm NOTIFY
connections the same timer sends
.Cm NOOP .
.Pp
TCP connect and each later IMAP read start at a 45 second limit.
A read timeout or a TCP timeout raises the limit for that server
and port.
The next limit is one and a half times the current one, at most
900 seconds, and it is stored in the learned file.
A stored value below 45 is ignored.
A successful command does not lower it.
A server
.Cm BYE ,
including
.Cm AccessTokenExpired ,
does not raise it.
Deleting the learned
.Va io
field returns that server to 45 seconds and keeps its NOTIFY and
ID record.
The running process notices that on its next stat.
The new limit is used on the next connect and the next read.
A dropped session does not raise the limit.
The TLS handshake uses the same deadline.
A handshake timeout does not raise it.
The socket is non-blocking.
While one connection waits, the other connections are polled
and
.Sy mbsync
can still start.
A wait that runs out drops that connection.
.Cm DONE
and each
.Cm STATUS
use one budget for the whole command, the server's current
read limit.
An untagged line, including
.Cm EXISTS ,
does not start that budget over.
A
.Cm DONE
that runs out fails and that connection is dropped.
A
.Cm STATUS
that runs out leaves the count unknown, and that mailbox is
synced.
Neither of those raises the learned limit.
.Pp
The first look at a host sends
.Dl ID ("name" "imapidle" "version" "0.3")
and
.Cm CAPABILITY
on the connection that then watches the account.
The ID reply is hashed.
A later connection does not send either command until that record
says it is due.
A changed hash requests
.Cm CAPABILITY
again and is logged once.
A host that advertises
.Cm IDLE
but not
.Cm NOTIFY ,
or that rejects
.Cm NOTIFY ,
is opened with
.Cm IDLE
after that.
A capability list with neither stays on the single connection above.
A learned line that does not record that list stays on
.Cm IDLE
until the list is seen.
Later connections do not repeat the discovery and do not send
.Cm NOTIFY
again.
A host that accepts
.Cm NOTIFY
is armed on each new connection.
That arming is logged when the record changes.
Delete the host's line in
.Pa learned
to probe it again.
The running process notices that on its next stat.
The primary connection also lists mailboxes when that list is due.
Extra
.Cm IDLE
connections do not.
.Pp
Priority is the minimum time between
.Sy mbsync
runs for one folder.
A folder that has never been included in a run, or whose minimum time
since that run has already passed, is ready: the next arrival starts
the 5 second channel wait described above.
A continuation or a tagged reply is logged and does not schedule a
run.
A server keepalive such as
.Ql * OK Still here
and the notice that
.Cm IDLE
was armed again are logged only with
.Fl v .
They do not schedule a run either.
.Pp
An arrival that comes in before the minimum time has passed is kept.
When that time expires, the folder is scheduled on the next wake.
That scheduling does not add another 5 seconds, unless the channel
already has a combine window open, in which case the folder joins it.
If the minimum time passes with no further arrival, the folder stays
quiet until new mail arrives or its resync time elapses.
For example,
.Cm INBOX
at priority 5 is included in a run, and another arrival for that
mailbox comes in during the following 420 seconds.
That arrival does not start a second run immediately.
When the 420 seconds end, the folder is scheduled.
If those 420 seconds pass quietly, the next arrival schedules the
run, after the 5 second wait.
The folder is also scheduled when its resync time has passed since
its last sync.
The default is 12 hours, and the priority gap still applies.
.Sy imapidlectl(1)
lists that last start and the time the resync interval next
elapses.
.Pp
A watched
.Cm IDLE
folder is synced when new mail arrives.
The server announces the message total with
.Cm EXISTS .
The log names the folder and the change from the remembered
total, for example
.Ql INBOX * 5265 EXISTS (+2 from last check) .
That total is remembered from the mailbox selection.
A dropped session keeps the total for that account and folder.
The next selection starts one sync when the new total is higher,
and does not when the total is the same or lower.
A later
.Cm EXISTS
starts a sync when the total is higher than the remembered number.
A removal is remembered as well, so one deletion and one arrival can
bring the announced total back to a familiar number and still start
a sync.
.Va hyperdelete
set to
.Cm yes
also starts a sync when a message is removed.
.Cm EXPUNGE
and
.Cm VANISHED
update the remembered total.
.Cm FETCH
and
.Cm RECENT
leave it unchanged.
Those four wait for the next arrival or the resync time.
A
.Cm NOTIFY
session still schedules each notice it was asked to watch.
.Pp
When an
.Cm IDLE
or poll
.Sy mbsync
of a watched folder exits 0, that folder is asked
.Cm STATUS
.Pq MESSAGES UIDNEXT
and those two numbers replace the ones that were remembered.
A higher message total schedules another sync, because mail may have
arrived after
.Sy mbsync
opened the mailbox.
A higher
.Cm UIDNEXT
with no higher total schedules one confirmation sync, so a deletion
and an arrival during the run are still fetched.
A confirmation that again moves only
.Cm UIDNEXT
stores the new value and does not schedule.
A flag change that rewrites the UID on every run cannot keep
.Sy mbsync
going.
A lower total is stored and left for the next arrival.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/imapidle/imapidle.conf
Default configuration.
See
.Sy imapidle.conf(5) .
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/imapidle
Boot script.
.Xr rcctl 8 Cm enable imapidle
and
.Cm start imapidle
run it as
.Va imapidle_user
from
.Pa /etc/rc.conf.local .
That account owns the configuration and the mailboxes.
.Va imapidle_flags
is passed through, and
.Va imapidle_timeout
defaults to 90 seconds.
.Cm reload
sends
.Dv SIGHUP
and
.Cm stop
sends
.Dv SIGTERM .
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/
State directory, mode 0700.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/tunnel/
Mode 0600 copies of the
.Sy mbsync
configuration, one per run, while
.Va tunnel
is on.
The file is removed when that
.Sy mbsync
exits.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/known_hosts
SHA256 certificate fingerprints.
A current line is
.Pa server:port
and the fingerprint.
A retired line adds the GMT time it was replaced, as
.Ql YYYYMMDDTHH:MM:SSZ .
One line per host has no time.
If this file is missing and the configuration directory has one, that
file is copied once.
The old path is not deleted.
The file mode is 0600 when
.Nm
creates it.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/certs/
One directory per local year.
Each file is one certificate chain from a TLS handshake.
The name is the SHA1 of the file, a hyphen, and
.Pa host:port ,
for example
.Pa 2026/f0cbd2da7e9abbcce6171027bd69f28165468420-mail0.fdh.bz:993 .
The text matches
.Cm openssl s_client -showcerts .
The same chain is not stored twice.
The file mode is 0600.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/cert-warn
The last time each
.Pa certs/
file produced a warning, as seconds since the epoch.
A line is that file's name and the time.
The file mode is 0600 when
.Nm
creates it.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/tls-negotiations/
One directory per local year.
Each file is what this client offered and what the server
selected.
The name is the SHA1 of the file, a hyphen, and
.Pa host:port .
Ephemeral keys, tickets, and session data are left out, so
the same server keeps the same file until the client or the
server changes the handshake.
The file mode is 0600.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/learned
What each server did with
.Cm NOTIFY ,
plus the ID hash and when to ask again.
A line is
.Pa server:port ,
then
.Cm absent ,
.Cm rejected ,
or
.Cm active .
A rejected or active server may add the name it reported.
Delete a line to probe that server again.
An older file beside the configuration is copied once, the same way
as
.Pa known_hosts .
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/folders/
One file per channel.
One mailbox name per line, as the server spelled it.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/track
The next wake, the interval, and
.Cm UIDNEXT
of each tracked mailbox, and when the next folder list is due.
Written on shutdown.
A later start uses those clocks.
The file mode is 0600 when
.Nm
creates it.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/mbsync-real
How many times each
.Sy mbsync
command has run, and the totals used on the stop line.
The key is the
.Cm sha1 -b
token of that command.
An older base64 key is still read.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/conn-time
How many times each IMAP session has ended, and the total seconds it
stayed connected.
The key is the same
.Cm sha1 -b
token.
An older base64 key is still read.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/pulse-last
The last time an
.Sy mbsync
of a pulse channel exited.
One channel and that time per line.
A mailbox already listed in
.Pa track
keeps its own clock.
A mailbox seen for the first time uses this time as the start
of its first wait, which is
.Va sweep
plus a quarter spread drawn for that mailbox.
.It Pa ~/tmp/pause
.It Pa ~/tmp/pause.mail
.It Pa ~/tmp/pause.imapidle
While any of these exists, no new
.Sy mbsync
is started.
.It Pa ~/tmp/shutdown
.It Pa ~/tmp/shutdown.mail
.It Pa ~/tmp/shutdown.imapidle
While any of these exists, open IMAP sessions are closed and the
process exits after
.Sy mbsync
finishes.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std imapidle
Startup failures exit 1 before the main loop.
That includes a missing configuration, a file with no account
and no pulse section, and a login that lacks
.Va server ,
.Va user ,
or exactly one of
.Va pass
and
.Va pass_cmd .
An
.Cm idle
section requires
.Va channel .
Login keys on an
.Cm idle
or
.Cm pulse
section reject the file.
An
.Cm account
section defaults an omitted
.Va mbsync_channel
to its own name.
The message is written to standard error.
The process does not fork.
After the main loop has started, the process exits 0 on
.Dv SIGTERM ,
.Dv SIGINT ,
or a shutdown file.
.Dv SIGHUP
reloads the configuration and does not exit.
.Dv SIGINFO
prints the running state and does not exit.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Watch the log on the terminal:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
imapidle -d
.Ed
.Pp
Use a configuration outside the default path:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
imapidle -c /home/user/.config/imapidle/imapidle.conf
.Ed
